title: Fairphone
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

A Dutch company trying to make a phone with fairer materials and labor, and one you can repair.

When its first phone launched in 2013 at €325, it published where the money went: about €185 on the phone itself, €45 on running the company, and about €22 on the things that were supposed to make it fair.

The same document said which figures were still estimates, and that its living-wage study wasn't finished.

A price tag that shows its own working, including the parts not done yet.

See [[Does a sign become more honest when its maker admits what it doesn't yet do?]], [[Design for Disassembly]], [[Build a Cellphone]]

